  "facts": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Alvarado is the oldest town in Johnson County, founded in the mid‑1800s and still retains its historic charm.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/alvarado-tx"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town was named after Alvarado, Veracruz, Mexico, where its founder fought during the Mexican–American War.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alvarado%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Railroads arrived in 1881, with the Gulf, Colorado and Santa Fe and the Missouri, Kansas and Texas lines boosting local growth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/alvarado-tx"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "By 1880 Alvarado boasted a bank, the Alvarado Bulletin newspaper, two schools, several cotton gins, a hotel, and an opera house.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/alvarado-tx"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Early settlers quickly established a post office, homes, businesses, and churches, laying the foundation for the community.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.johnsoncountytx.org/departments/museum/alvarado"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Alvarado Park Lake, a reservoir on Turkey Creek, sits just three miles southwest of the city and is a local recreation spot.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
